The attached image (Figure 9 in the PDF) illustrates that Nano-11 significantly enhances the antigen-specific antibody response. It shows anti-recombinant protective antigen (rPA) IgG titers in mice immunized with rPA alone or formulated with various doses of aluminum hydroxide (AH) or Nano-11. All Nano-11 formulations (12.5, 50, 200 µg/dose) induced significantly higher IgG titers compared to rPA alone, achieving levels comparable to or exceeding those with AH. This demonstrates Nano-11's strong adjuvant effect in enhancing antigen-specific humoral immunity in vivo.

The dendrimer-like alpha-D-glucan nanoparticles (Nano-11) described could significantly contribute to cancer research by serving as an effective adjuvant for therapeutic cancer vaccines. Nano-11's ability to activate dendritic cells, enhance antigen uptake and delivery to draining lymph nodes, and induce robust antigen-specific antibody responses suggests it could boost the immunogenicity of tumor-associated antigens (TAAs). This could lead to more potent anti-tumor immune responses, improving the efficacy of cancer immunotherapies by eliciting stronger and more durable humoral and cellular immunity.